const patch1170 = `type: ADD
date_requested: &1 2018-12-15
date_active: *1
ob_issue_no: '1170'
identifier:
  code: 1170-001
data:
  _class: E212MNCAmendment
  entries:
  - country_or_area: {en: Hungary SUP}        # a printed section heading row
  - mcc_mnc_codes: 216 04
    networks: Invitech ICT Services Ltd.      # the item that the action inserts
  # (the file continues with a Kenya section and its item)
  notes:
  - "*MCC:  Mobile Country Code / Indicatif de pays du mobile / Indicativo de país para el servicio móvil"`;
---
<Base
  title="How it works"
  masthead
  description="How the ITU Operational Bulletin's printed changes become a replayable record over the international telecommunication registers."
>
  <article class="hw">
    <header class="hw-head">
      <p class="hw-eyebrow">The model of the Bulletin</p>
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p class="hw-lede">
        The Operational Bulletin of the International Telecommunication Union
        (ITU) publishes, in each issue, the changes that administrations and
        operators make to the international telecommunication registers: a
        charge card issuer is added, a mobile network code is withdrawn, a
        signalling point is reassigned. A reader who requires the current
        composition of a register must otherwise assemble that composition from
        hundreds of printed issues, and a reader who requires the reason for an
        entry must locate the issue that introduced it.
      </p>
      <p class="hw-lede">
        This site answers that need by keeping the changes themselves as data.
        Every change that the Bulletin prints for a register is recorded as a
        typed action, the actions are collected in a reviewed repository, and
        the registers that this site presents are computed from those actions.
        This page explains the system by following one real action, the
        addition of the mobile network code <span class="num font-mono">216 04</span>, from the printed page to the
        current register.
      </p>
    </header>

    {/* Figure: the life of an item */}
    <figure class="hw-life" aria-label="The life of an item, in four steps">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">1 · The printed line</span>
          <div class="hw-art">
            <p class="hw-print">Hungary — SUP</p>
            <p class="hw-print"><span class="num font-mono">216 04</span> Invitech ICT Services Ltd.</p>
            <p class="hw-cap">The line is printed in OB № 1170 of 15.IV.2019.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">2 · The patch file</span>
          <div class="hw-art hw-art-code">
            <pre class="num font-mono">type: ADD
ob_issue_no: '1170'</pre>
            <p class="hw-cap">The action occupies one file in the patch repository.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">3 · The replayed state</span>
          <div class="hw-art">
            <p class="hw-print num font-mono">seed, then actions, in issue order</p>
            <p class="hw-cap">The replay reconstructs the register as it stood at issue № 1170.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="hw-step">4 · The item today</span>
          <div class="hw-art">
            <p class="hw-print"><span class="num font-mono">216 04</span> Invitech ICT Services Ltd.</p>
            <p class="hw-cap">The item is present in the current edition, together with its recorded history.</p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </figure>

    <section>
      <h2 id="registers"><span class="hw-no num">1</span> Recommendations define registers; registers hold items</h2>
      <p>
        The International Telecommunication Union publishes its registers
        under Recommendations, which are the normative source for what a
        register contains. One Recommendation can define more than one
        register: Recommendation ITU-T E.164 defines three registers (the
        country code list, the assignments list, and the dial procedures
        list), and Recommendation ITU-T Q.708 defines two registers (the
        international signalling point codes (ISPC) and the signalling
        area/network codes (SANC)). This site carries 17 registers in total.
      </p>
      <p>
        A register is a set of items, where one item is one charge card
        issuer, one mobile network assignment, or one signalling point. The
        items of a register share a single shape, which is called the <em>data schema</em> of the register, and each item carries the key of
        the register: the issuer identifier number for E.118, the mobile
        country code/mobile network code (MCC/MNC) pair for E.212, and the
        data network identification code (DNIC) for X.121. An item that
        carries a key can be named by the register, and an item that can be
        named is an item whose insertion, removal and modification can be
        recorded.
      </p>
      <div class="hw-item hw-art">
        <p class="hw-cap">The item <span class="num font-mono">216 04</span> in the current edition of the E.212 register.</p>
        <p class="num font-mono">216 04 — Invitech ICT Services Ltd.</p>
        <p class="hw-cap">The key of the register is the MCC/MNC pair, and the item has <a href="/registers/e212-mnc/entries/216%2004/">its own addressable page</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="issues"><span class="hw-no num">2</span> An issue is a set of messages; a register message is an action</h2>
      <p>
        Each issue of the Bulletin carries messages of many kinds, including
        running annexes, approved Recommendations, service restrictions,
        telephone service notices, and amendments to the registers. The
        amendments are the messages that change data. Each amendment is an <em>action</em> on exactly one register: the action inserts an item,
        removes an item, or changes an item, and the item is named by the key
        of the register.
      </p>
      <table class="hw-kinds">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Action</th><th>Operation</th><th>Payload</th><th>Example</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="hw-chip hw-chip-add num font-mono">ADD</span></td>
            <td>inserts an item</td>
            <td>the complete new item</td>
            <td><a href="/issues/1170/">216 04, in issue № 1170</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="hw-chip hw-chip-sup num font-mono">SUP</span></td>
            <td>removes an item</td>
            <td>the key of the item</td>
            <td><a href="/registers/e118-iin/entries/89%20351%2007/">89 351 07, in issue № 1162</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="hw-chip hw-chip-mod num font-mono">REP</span></td>
            <td>changes an item</td>
            <td>the key and the new values</td>
            <td><a href="/registers/x121-dnic/entries/440%201/">440 1, in issue № 1129</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Two further kinds complete the vocabulary. An action of kind <span class="num font-mono">LIR</span> carries a row that the printed Bulletin repeats from the previous edition of the list, and an action of kind <span class="num font-mono">SEED</span> establishes the initial complete list of a register. The seed is the only kind whose payload is a whole item set, because an initial state has no earlier state from which a difference could be taken.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="patch"><span class="hw-no num">3</span> The patch log is the record</h2>
      <p>
        Action 1170-001 exists as a small file in the <a href="https://github.com/ituob/itu-ob-data" rel="external">patch repository</a>, which holds one file per action and is reviewed through pull requests in the same manner as source code. The listing below shows the complete patch, abridged for display; the omissions are marked in the file comments.
      </p>
      <figure class="hw-patch">
        <pre class="num font-mono">{patch1170}</pre>
        <figcaption>
          In the listing, <dfn>type</dfn> carries the kind of the action, <dfn>ob_issue_no</dfn> names the issue that printed the change, <dfn>identifier</dfn> records the position that the Bulletin printed for the action, <dfn>entries</dfn> holds the items, and <dfn>notes</dfn> preserves the printed footnotes verbatim.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The patch files are the single source of truth of the program. The
        patches can always regenerate the complete list of a register at any
        point in time, while a complete list cannot be converted back into
        patches, because the information about which changes produced a state
        does not exist in the state itself. The complete files that this site
        publishes are therefore <em>projections</em> of the patch log: the
        build pipeline produces them, no person edits them, and they are
        never the input from which new changes are derived. A correction to
        published data is recorded as a new action, never as a rewrite of an
        existing one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="replay"><span class="hw-no num">4</span> Replay produces any date, on demand</h2>
      <p>
        The state of a register is computed rather than stored. The replay
        engine reads the seed, which is the initial complete list of the
        register, and applies the actions in issue order, which produces the
        state of the register after any chosen issue. For this reason every
        register page presents the current edition and every past edition.
      </p>
      <p>
        The seed of the E.212 register was established at issue № 1162, and
        162 issues have modified that register since. The E.118 register
        contains 940 items in its current edition; these items are rebuilt
        from 355 recorded actions across 123 issues. The edition of E.118 at
        issue № 1170 and the complete change history of the register are
        available for inspection.
      </p>
      <p>
        Readers who wish to examine the mechanism directly can <a href="/registers/e118-iin/at/1170/">open an edition from 1170</a> or read <a href="/registers/e118-iin/history/">the change history of the register</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="projections"><span class="hw-no num">5</span> The files on the site are projections</h2>
      <p>
        The complete lists that the site renders, the point-in-time snapshots
        behind the edition pages, and the per-register dataset files are
        projections of the patch log, and the build pipeline regenerates all
        of them. Each projection states its provenance, which is the issue
        that it reflects. No projection is edited directly. When a projection
        and a fresh replay disagree, the disagreement is a defect in the
        pipeline rather than a matter of judgement.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="proofs"><span class="hw-no num">6</span> Two proofs run continuously</h2>
      <p>
        Two proofs run on every build. First, every item validates against the data schema of its register, and every action validates against the message schemas; both kinds of schema are declared in a single ontology (<span class="num font-mono">messages.lml</span>, 86 classes). The corpus validation covered 16,834 files at the latest run, and the validators reject unknown fields instead of ignoring them. Second, the outputs themselves are verified: rebuilt snapshots must match a fresh replay, and the parsers are compared against captured baselines to establish equivalence. Content that would drift from the printed Bulletin fails the build.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="repos"><span class="hw-no num">7</span> Where the parts live</h2>
      <table class="hw-repos">
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ituob/itu-ob-data" rel="external">itu-ob-data</a></th>
            <td>The repository holds the patch log, which contains every action that was ever printed, each reviewed through a pull request.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ituob/service-publications-docs" rel="external">service-publications-docs</a></th>
            <td>The repository contains the parsers, the replay engine, the schema ontology, and the proof gates.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><a href="https://github.com/ituob/ituob.github.io" rel="external">ituob.github.io</a></th>
            <td>The repository contains this site, which is built statically from the replayed registers and served by a Worker.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="hw-end">
        Any statement on this page can be checked against the data. Select a
        register in <a href="/registers/">the registers index</a>, open an
        item, and read its change history back to the issue that printed it.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

